owing_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2014-09-22T05:10:31Z","updated_at":"2014-09-22T05:10:31Z","body":"Hmm, this has the problem that if a program redefines `staticmethod`, it is still recognized as a static method. It's a pretty weird corner case, I know... However, `visit_decorator` in `mypy/semanal.py` detects `staticmethod` decorators, and it can deal with weird things like this:\n\n```\ndef staticmethod(f):\n    return f\n\nclass A:\n    @staticmethod   # not actually a static method\n    def m(self): ...\n```\n\nand this:\n\n```\nfrom builtins import staticmethod as foo\n\nclass A:\n    @foo   # this actually is a static method\n    def m(self): ...\n```\n\nThe signature of static methods should probably be tweaked in the semantic analyzer to remain consistent with the rest of the implementation. Let me know if you'd like to have more pointers.\n","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python/mypy/issues/comments/56329383/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"JukkaL","id":1107911,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExMDc5MTE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1107911?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JukkaL","html_url":"https://github.com/JukkaL","followers_url":"https://api.g